  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research the primary asymptotic replacement: Kitamasa-style polynomial exponentiation in src/npbonacci.cpp. Use the characteristic relation x^n = P1*x^(n-1) + ... + Pn. Compute x^(H-1) modulo this polynomial, then dot its coefficients in ascending degree with A1,...,An. This preserves the input's lag ordering without constructing matrices. Target O(n^2 log H) time and O(n) storage using convolution followed by descending-degree reduction.\n\nEstablish a compact, single-threaded implementation first. Handle n=1 by reducing x correctly rather than accessing coefficient index 1. Then measure squaring symmetry and bounded modular accumulation. Store normalized residues compactly, widen before multiplication, and prove accumulator bounds; an entire length-1020 dot product cannot safely accumulate unreduced in 64 bits. Deliver a fully correct, measured candidate without compiler or harness changes.",
        "B": "Research an independent recurrence solver using Bostan-Mori and ordinary exact modular convolution. Define Q(z)=1-P1*z-...-Pn*z^n and S_i=A_(i+1)-sum_{j=1..i}(Pj*A_(i-j+1)) modulo MOD for 0<=i<n. The required answer is coefficient H-1 of S/Q.\n\nAt each step form R(z)=Q(-z), multiply S*R and Q*R, retain numerator coefficients whose parity matches the current index, retain even denominator coefficients, and halve the index. The denominator's constant coefficient remains 1, so the final answer is S_0. Normalize all subtractions. Target O(n^2 log H) time and O(n) storage initially, then exploit parity and symmetry to avoid computing discarded coefficients. Compare output-oriented convolution kernels and only test threading when substantial work amortizes its overhead. This should be an independently correct alternative to A, not a presumed improvement.",
        "C": "Research low-overhead execution and short-horizon inputs. Implement direct recurrence evaluation with a ring buffer in O(n*(H-n)) time and O(n) space; this is especially valuable when H is close to n. Any selection rule must depend on general workload dimensions, not particular benchmark values.\n\nFor a complete independent alternative, simplify the existing matrix method: start with state [An,...,A1], exponent e=H-n, apply selected companion-matrix powers directly to that vector, and square the matrix only while exponent bits remain. Eliminate identity/result matrices, the mostly-zero base matrix, fixed-capacity matrix copies, and the final matrix-by-matrix multiplication. Use compact active-size storage and investigate contiguous/transposed multiplication access with overflow-safe modular batching. This remains cubic for squaring, so treat it as a small-work candidate rather than the large-n default. Prioritize serial latency; compare limited threading only under the existing build configuration.",
        "G": "Integrate measured programs from A, B and C, not a speculative union of their ideas. First compare each complete valid candidate against the baseline using the unchanged automatic correctness and timing procedure. Choose the strongest general solver, then separately measure adding C's direct-recurrence path, modular kernels, or workload-based dispatch. Retain combinations only when their own measurements justify them; gains are not additive.\n\nPay particular attention to startup, allocation and thread-launch costs at the observed approximately 20 ms baseline. Use at most the available four CPU cores and avoid nested parallelism. Review exponent conventions carefully: A/B use H-1 with chronological initial terms, whereas C's matrix-vector method uses H-n with reversed state. Require correctness for n=1, short horizons, zero coefficients and maximum legal residues, with exactly one modular integer printed. Preserve tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target and permissions; never dispatch on expected answers or specific test values.",
        "analysis": "Only Problem C and src/npbonacci.cpp are relevant. The source uses dense companion-matrix exponentiation, cubic multiplication with an inner-loop modulus, large fixed-capacity arrays, and full result/base matrices despite needing one scalar. The baseline passed four correctness cases and averaged 0.01960 seconds across three timing samples; no optimized candidates, workload dimensions or profiling evidence are supplied. Therefore prioritize exact recurrence algorithms, while independently investigating short-horizon and low-overhead execution. ARM64 with four CPU cores permits modest parallelism, but the measured runtime makes synchronization overhead important. No particular input distribution or additive integration gain should be assumed."
      },
      "request_sha256": "96b5365c82a8abb5317cdd458aac11c09d92909fe75d102ed10298d5628008aa",
